WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.980
Good morning Hank, it's Friday.

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:05.300
So our tour ended last night in Los Angeles. Almost every night for the last month

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:08.660
I've stood at the side of the stage during your set and watched you play,

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:11.160
because I love your music and I love watching you perform it

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:13.360
and also because it brings back memories.

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:18.100
I think about the first time I saw you play in front of a crowd, 45 people in Grand Rapids, Michigan,

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:24.760
almost a decade ago now, or I think about other nights: Edinburgh; the first VidCon or 8808 in Chicago

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:30.200
when Harry Potter fans were singing your song so loudly I could barely hear your voice over theirs.

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:36.120
And I think about how later that night in 2008, we hung out with Amy Krauss Rosenthal, my friend who died earlier this year.

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:38.380
Here's an Amy story I've been telling on tour:

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:43.100
so, back in 2005, Amy, who was a children's book writer and memoirist and

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:47.080
professional seeker of wonder in the world invited me to one of her readings.

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:50.380
And at the time I was in one of my periods of intense unwellness,

00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:53.920
I felt like I was losing my whole self to intrusive thought spirals,

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:58.420
but this particular episode was accompanied by a chest crushing depression.

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:02.080
I found nothing enjoyable, I struggled to get out of bed, the usual.

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:06.960
It was at once absolutely excruciating and completely boring.

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:11.620
Susan Sontag wrote that "depression is melancholy, minus its charms".

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:17.280
Somehow I managed to get to the event but I wasn't having much fun at it because I was alone and I was stuck with these

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:21.260
invasive thoughts I couldn't stop having and also Amy kept wanting people to

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:25.300
participate in what she was doing and I hate audience participation!

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:32.100
It just makes me very nervous because: 1) sometimes the audience doesn't deliver and also 2) I have paid for the show to be entertained,

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:34.340
not to be part of the entertainment.

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:36.329
So anyway, I was just in a terrible mood,

00:01:36.329 --> 00:01:37.840
and then seemingly out of nowhere

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:41.520
Amy started talking about how back in World War I all of these British

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:48.320
soldiers who didn't understand why they were being asked to fight and die for tiny patches of ground far from their home started singing

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:51.460
a song to the tune of that New Year's Eve song, Auld Lang Syne.

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:57.720
They would sing "we're here because we're here because we're here because we're here".

00:01:57.720 --> 00:01:59.320
It was this recursive lament,

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:05.080
an acknowledgment that there was no "why", that life in the trenches was meaninglessness all the way down.

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:11.740
But that night in 2005 Amy completely transformed that song for me without ever changing the words.

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:17.920
Amy made me understand that WE were here, meaning that we were together and even when we felt alone,

00:02:18.020 --> 00:02:23.100
we weren't really, because we were part of this vast and deeply interconnected US.

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:29.160
And also that we were HERE, even if only for a little while. And maybe we'll never know why we are here,

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:34.559
but we can still proclaim in hope THAT we are here. That night Amy made me understand that

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:38.880
hope is not foolish or idealistic or misguided, hope is true.

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:40.120
And I really believe that.

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:43.960
I really believe that hope is the correct response to the arc of history.

00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:48.300
Hank, some years are longer than others and 2017 has been a long one for me.

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:55.240
It's been good and bad and difficult and amazing and joyful and terrifying and very long.

00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:57.740
But we're here. We're here.

00:02:57.880 --> 00:02:59.880
Hank, I'll see you on Monday.

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:03.100
- And now, Hank, will you?

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:04.880
- Yeah, I'm extremely scared.

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:07.260
This is it, somebody has a laptop with an internet connection?

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:09.220
I'm getting kind of tired of this

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:14.800
Prepublication media blitz
You've got all of Muggle kind under your spell

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:23.200
Don't you know the whole world's already gone
And reserved a copy at Amazon
How many more books could you sell?

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:28.940
Now give me my book or go to hell!

00:03:28.940 --> 00:03:37.300
'Cause I need Harry Potter
Like a grindylow needs water
And as Saturday approaches my need grows

00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:45.760
Accio Deathly Hallows
Incendio book sales embargoes;
It'll be like phoenix tears on a broken nose

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:49.620
Accio Deathly Hallows

